VIEW(1)                        P32W Unix                         VIEW(1)

NAME
       view - look at a picture or at text, in a window

SYNOPSIS
       view file

DESCRIPTION
       view shows a file: a picture if it is one, and its text if
       not. Under the window manager (wm(1)) it has a window of
       its own; with no window manager it takes the whole of the
       video card's screen (video(4)).

       A picture is a PBM file of the packed kind: it begins P4,
       then its width and height, then its dots a line at a time,
       the top line first, eight dots to a byte, a 1 a black dot
       (a dot that is not lit). wm's Snapshot writes such files,
       and so do programs on other machines. Its window is the
       picture's size, 640 by 440 dots at most. Where the picture
       is bigger than what shows, h, j, k and l (or the arrows)
       move over it: left, down, up and right, 64 dots at a time.

       Text is shown 24 lines at a time, in a window 78 columns
       wide, a longer line folded onto the next. The keys:

       space, f       a page on
       b              a page back
       j, Return, k   a line on, a line back (and the arrows)
       g, G           the beginning, the end

       and under wm a press of the mouse on the bar at the right:
       on its upper half a page back, on its lower half a page
       on. The box in the bar is as much of the file as shows.

       q ends it, or its window's box.

EXAMPLES
       win view /usr/examples/README
       win view screen1.pbm
       view notes
              from a terminal's shell: the terminal waits until
              it ends

       files(1) shows a file with it when the file is opened.

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/view.c        its source

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), win(1), files(1), more(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       view: file: not a picture this can show, for a P4 file
       whose size is not given, or is above 4096 either way; it
       ends before its last line, when the file is short (what
       there is of it is shown).

BUGS
       Characters that are not printing ASCII are shown as dots.
       A picture's colours cannot be turned over.
